Subject: [PATCH 2/2] Connect chunk and object transitions to retained lifetime authority
Upstream: memcached 1.6.45
Upstream-Archive: https://memcached.org/files/memcached-1.6.45.tar.gz
Upstream-SHA256: d362c64e6d8d5287153501eabf7c85b4a761432fbf53f5d7b085d0bb1653c1dd
Build-Variants: native port, Capstone domain
Depends-On: memcached-1.6.45-0001-freestanding-shims.patch
Apply-From: upstream source root, with patch --batch --forward --fuzz=0 -p1

Slabs never frees a chunk. do_slabs_free pushes an item on its class's slots
list and do_slabs_alloc pops it straight back; a page leaves a class only
through the mover, never through free(). cache.c is the same shape one level
down: an object is malloc'd once, pushed on a STAILQ at cache_free and popped
uncleared at cache_alloc, freed only over a limit or at destroy. A hook on
free() would therefore never fire on the paths that matter. The hooks sit on
the transitions instead: mcp_chunk_release where do_slabs_free and
do_slabs_free_chunked file a chunk, after its header has been read and before
its links are written; mcp_chunk_issue where do_slabs_alloc pops one; and
mcp_object_release / mcp_object_issue at the two corresponding lines of
cache.c.

Each returns the alias upstream must use from then on. In the protected mode
the adapter revokes the chunk's authority and mints a fresh alias at each
transition, so a pointer held across slabs_free is dead rather than rebound to
whatever item the chunk becomes next. Pages come from the payload region in
place of memory_allocate's malloc, and once do_slabs_newslab has zeroed a page
the whole-page alias is exchanged for one region per chunk; the split then
asks for each chunk by number, because a pointer stepped past its own chunk is
not the next chunk's alias. The slab list grows element-wise, since it holds
page aliases. cache.c's control block and name come from the metadata heap,
so an allocator never lives inside storage it hands out.

Upstream keeps every decision: the class table, perslab, the LIFO order, which
chunk the next slabs_alloc pops, which object the next cache_alloc pops.
10 hunks in slabs.c and 5 in cache.c, 19 lines removed and 46 added; the
mover, which is not built, is not touched.

